\subsection{Factorial Interpretation}
\label{sec:mechanism}

\textbf{Gradient flow is associated with lower safety under BatchNorm but not under LayerNorm.} The BN row provides the clearest signal: Full OCC (BN) is the sole factorial cell falling below Vanilla ($\Delta{=}-2.0$\,pp, $g{=}-0.34$ vs.\ Vanilla), whereas Detach OCC recovers above it ($\Delta{=}+2.2$\,pp, $g{=}+0.74$, large). In the LN row, Full OCC+LN already exceeds Vanilla ($0.773$, $g{=}+0.90$, large), and Detach+LN yields the highest cell mean ($0.802$, $g{=}+1.89$, very large; bootstrap 95\% CI for $\Delta$: $[0.025, 0.089]$; Welch $t$ $p{=}0.045$). This is a practical threshold crossing---only BN + gradient flow is associated with reduced safety---rather than a statistical interaction (the factorial interaction is negligible, $\eta^2{=}0.005$). The numerical advantage of Detach OCC ($+$2.2\,pp, with 3 of 4 seeds exceeding the Vanilla mean) raises the possibility that detached auxiliary heads provide a mild regularization benefit, though larger samples are needed to confirm.

\textbf{Temporal dynamics.} Cutoff conditions illuminate when gradient flow's association with lower safety emerges. Cutoff@15K ($\Delta{=}+0.2$\,pp) performs near Vanilla, while Cutoff@25K shows a large deficit ($\Delta{=}-5.6$\,pp) and Cutoff@35K returns to baseline ($\Delta{=}-0.1$\,pp). Cutoff@45K ($\Delta{=}-1.6$\,pp, single seed) falls between these extremes. This non-monotonic pattern suggests that the critical window for gradient flow damage is not simply cumulative exposure but depends on the training phase. Reverse Cutoff (gradient flow only after step 35K) produces the worst supplementary result ($\Delta{=}-6.2$\,pp), indicating that late-stage gradient flow---when the model is refining action predictions---is associated with substantially worse outcomes than early exposure during initial representation learning. However, temporal conditions use 1--3 seeds under v1, so these observations are preliminary; targeted replication under v2 with more seeds would be needed to confirm the temporal pattern.

\subsection{Gradient Dynamics}

Gradient analysis (Figure~\ref{fig:gradient_analysis}) examines the mechanism through which auxiliary heads affect safety. All gradient norms are computed with respect to the L1 action loss (action tokens only).

