\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The permutation test yields stronger evidence for both main effects ($p_\text{perm}{=}0.050$ and $0.018$) than the parametric $F$-test ($p{=}0.104$ and $0.060$), consistent with the parametric test being conservative for small, non-normal samples. The interaction remains negligible ($p_\text{perm}{=}0.774$), confirming the additive pattern. The normalization main effect reaches significance at $\alpha{=}0.05$ under permutation.

\section{Per-Task Factorial Analysis}
\label{app:per_task_factorial}

The three physically constrained tasks (T3: \texttt{cookie\_box}, T5: \texttt{ramekin}, T6: \texttt{next\_to\_cookie\_box}) amplify both main effects by approximately $3{\times}$ relative to the overall means.

\textbf{Normalization effect amplification.} The overall normalization effect ($\Delta{=}+3.5$--$4.8$\,pp between BN and LN) is amplified on constrained tasks. The LN ${>}$ BN direction is consistent across all three constrained tasks.

\textbf{Gradient flow on constrained tasks.} On T5 (ramekin), the best auxiliary condition versus Vanilla yields $g{=}2.12$, the largest per-task effect size observed. T5 also shows a reversed gradient flow direction ($g{=}+1.05$, ON ${>}$ OFF), but this reflects near-floor performance (CAR $0.04$--$0.19$) where absolute differences are small and floor artifacts are expected.

These per-task patterns suggest that the safety-relevant design choices (normalization type, gradient flow) are disproportionately consequential on physically constrained tasks where collision avoidance is most difficult.

